        "full_sha": "aa7305f47250a30494eec505ad6b1a7d4057bb54",
        "date": "2026-07-08",
        "author": "mpfaffenberger",
        "subject": "fix(shell): isolate Windows shell children from our console",
        "body": "Root cause of the ^C-during-shell brick: foreground shell commands inherited our console AND our stdin. Probe results (real console, verified live): both 'timeout /t' and even 'powershell -Command Start-Sleep' re-enable ENABLE_PROCESSED_INPUT on the shared console input buffer via the inherited stdin handle -- so every shell command opened a window where ^C fired console-wide CTRL_C_EVENTs (killing uvx.exe, waking the parent shell into fighting us for stdin). timeout /t also literally consumed the user's keystrokes ('press a key to continue'). The key listener's 1s re-clamp can't win a mode tug-of-war against a live child.",
        "theme": "tools",
        "kind": "fix",
        "impact": "notable",
        "summary": {
          "impact": "notable",
          "what": "Root cause of the ^C-during-shell brick: foreground shell commands inherited our console AND our stdin.",
          "why_rad": "Probe results (real console, verified live): both 'timeout /t' and even 'powershell -Command Start-Sleep' re-enable ENABLE_PROCESSED_INPUT on the shared console input buffer via the inherited stdin handle -- so every shell command opened a window where ^C fired console-wide CTRL_C_EVENTs (killing uvx.exe, waking the parent shell into fighting us for stdin).",
          "how_helps": "The key listener's 1s re-clamp can't win a mode tug-of-war against a live child."
        },
        "files": [
          "pyproject.toml",
          "uv.lock"
        ],
